In this lesson we'll look at how to design a simulation for a chance experiment. A simulation is basically a pretend version of the real thing, run using a random number generator like a die, a coin, your spreadsheet's random function, or some code. First, you spot which parts of the real experiment are random. Next, you map each possible outcome onto a number, so for example rolling a one, two, or three on a die might stand for red, while a four or five stands for blue. Then you run that pretend experiment heaps of times, because the more runs you do, the more reliable your estimate becomes. After each run, you record what you're interested in, like how often red came up. Finally, you estimate the probability by dividing the number of times the event happened by the total number of runs, giving you a relative frequency. So the recipe is simple: identify, map, run, record, estimate. Now let's see it in action.